Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: "Who Salted the Date-Syrup Kettle?"
- Story & Context: During a windless trade-fair at the dry well of Bir-Qattara, caravan cook Jamila dropped an iron ladle of scalding date-syrup directly into a wooden crate containing a salvaged 1958 tube amplifier and an unspooled tape deck. Instead of extinguishing the circuit, the sticky sugar glazed across the hot vacuum tubes, locking the mechanism into a warm, saturated oscillation that looped at precisely 172 beats per minute through the market's horn speakers. Vespera grabbed an oxidized ribbon microphone from a scrap blanket and began to wail an impromptu lament over the sweet-smelling, smoke-belching gear as merchants paused with their bowls raised: "You left me parched at the table while the copper pot boiled dry."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A close-up cinematic shot of thick, amber date syrup dripping slowly over glowing vintage amplifier vacuum tubes and brass circuit boards resting on desert sand, illuminated by flickers of firelight, surreal psychedelic steam rising in geometric curls.

Track 2: Mutton Tallow Smothers the Ribbon Mic
- Story & Context: Trapped inside a felt tent at Camp Malha while a razor-sharp dust storm stripped the enamel off the supply wagons, synth-mechanic Farid confessed his desert betrayal over a sputtering camping stove. He used grease from rendered mutton ribs to free the seized pitch-wheels of an ancient patchbay synth, whispering how he had gambled away the caravan’s water rations to buy a contraband crate of mid-century studio preamps from a rival convoy. As the smell of seared meat fat filled the tent, Vespera recorded his trembling admission directly through a grease-stained microphone casing, letting the sorrow dissolve into a rushing torrent of sub-bass and glistening liquid breakbeats: "I poured our water into the circuitry just to hear you say my name."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: An artistic, moody editorial frame of a vintage 1950s pill-shaped ribbon microphone resting beside an iron frying pan full of sizzling tallow on an open gas burner, fine desert sand swirling in the soft golden shadows around exposed audio jacks.

Track 3: The Baker of Qanat-Sidi
- Story & Context: Decades after the great trade routes dissolved into legend, travelers visiting the clay bread-ovens of Qanat-Sidi discovered that the mud-brick flues still sang whenever the noon thermals swept through the hearths. The village baker had lined the subterranean baking pits with the ceramic circuit boards of discarded five-pin modular synths, unwittingly turning the ovens into acoustic resonators that tuned the roar of burning tamarisk wood to a roaring sub-bass vibration. Now, as the dough rises on flat river stones, the entire bakery hums with a ghostly, pitch-drifting torch ballad that floats across the dunes like a baker's benediction: "Break the crust with strangers before the current turns to rust."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A surreal, mystical view inside a glowing earthen bread oven, where round loaves of flatbread bake atop ancient, heat-scorched synthesizer boards and vacuum tubes embedded in the clay, emitting soft cyan sparks through wisps of white flour dust.

